## $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and surface area measurements
## Single crystal and powder diffraction
Additional NPD measurements of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loaded $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ were performed on the high-intensity neutron powder diffractometer, WOMBAT,45 also located at the Bragg Institute at ANSTO. The WOMBAT instrument was congured with a Ge(113) monochromator with a take-off angle of $110^{\circ}$ with open primary and secondary collimation, resulting in a wavelength of 2.7948 A˚. Data were collected at select temperatures upon cooling. Temperatures ranged between 10 and $300\mathrm{~K~}$ for the bare $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ framework and then again with sequential loadings of $0.5\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ and $1.5\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules per iron. For each loading, the cryostat and sample were heated to $300~\mathrm{K}$ to dose $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ gas (Fig. S22 and $\mathbf{S}23\dagger$ ).
All NPD data were analyzed using the Rietveld method as implemented in EXPGUI/GSAS.46,47 The activated $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ model was rened with most structural and peak prole parameters free to vary, resulting in a structure very similar to that determined using single crystal X-ray diffraction. Atomic displacement parameters (ADPs) for atoms with the same identity were constrained to be the same throughout the renement process. Fourier difference analysis, applied to data obtained from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed samples, was then employed to locate the adsorbed molecules in the frameworks. With the exception of the Fe and Mg analogs, intramolecular bond distances and angles of the adsorbed molecules were initially restrained to values expected for a free $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule. Once renements became stable, the values were allowed to vary freely. The modeled atoms within each $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule were constrained to maintain the same fractional occupancies throughout the renement. For analysis of the large number of lower resolution data sets collected on WOMBAT, sequential renements were carried out using the GSAS/EXPGUI46,47 so- ware package. First, Rietveld renement of a single data set was carried out manually and then the parameters obtained from the rst data set were used as a starting point for the subsequent and automatic renement of the remaining data sets.
In an attempt to elucidate the global minimum structure for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , data was collected at $100\mathrm{~K~}$ on the XSD rapid-acquisition powder beamline, 17-BM-B, at the Advanced Photon Source (APS) at Argonne National Laboratory. Desolvated $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ with a mass of approximately $9\ \mathrm{mg}$ was loaded into a quartz capillary in a ${\bf N}_{2}$ -purged glove box. The capillary was attached to a custom gas-dosing cell equipped with a gas valve. $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ was further activated in situ by heating the capillary at $380~\mathrm{K}$ for $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ under dynamic vacuum provided via a turbomolecular pump. The samples was cooled to room temperature, dosed with approximately $1.5\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , and slowly cooled to $100\mathrm{~K~}$ for data collection. 17-BM-B has an incident energy range of $15{-}18\ \mathrm{keV}$ and calibrated cycle wavelength of $0.72959\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ (Fig. S31 and Table S26†).
For single crystal structural analysis, a methanol solvated crystal of $\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ was mounted on a Kapton loop using a minimal amount of epoxy applied only on faces that propagate in directions parallel to the channel direction (along the long axis of the rod-shaped crystals). The sample was then placed in a custom-made gas cell equipped with a gas loading valve and a borosilicate glass cap sealed with an O-ring. The cell was evacuated under reduced pressure at $473\mathrm{K}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to remove solvent molecules. Data was then collected at Beamline 11.3.1 at the Advanced Light Source, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory using synchrotron radiation $\big(\lambda=0.77490\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{\big)}^{}$ . A Bruker AXS APEX II diffractometer was used for data collection, and the corresponding Bruker AXS APEX II soware was used for data collection and reduction.48 The structure of the desolvated crystal was obtained from a preliminary data set to conrm the absence of solvent bound at the Co sites. The cell was then warmed to room temperature and dosed with 6.7 mbar of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . The cell was subsequently cooled at a rate of $2\mathrm{K}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ to a nal temperature of $150\mathrm{~K~}$ . The crystal was found to be an obverse/ reverse twin based on the diffraction pattern. The program
## Inelastic neutron scattering
Inelastic neutron scattering (INS) experiments were performed on $\phantom{-}0.7057\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ employing the Filter Analyzer Neutron Spectrometer (FANS) at the NCNR.54 Spectra were obtained by illuminating the samples with a collimated and monochromated neutron beam. Aer passing through a low-energy band-pass lter consisting of Bi, Be, and graphite, the energy transfer of the scattered neutrons was determined at a bank of $^3\mathrm{He}$ detectors. Data were rst collected for the bare framework, followed by data collection for the framework loaded with 0.75 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per Fe. Gas was loaded into the framework using the same methods as described for the high-resolution NPD experiments. The spectra of the bare framework were then subtracted from the spectra obtained for the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loaded sample using the DAVE suite of programs (Fig. ${\bf S}24\dagger$ ).55 This process was repeated on $0.834\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{(dobdc-}d_{2})$ ) using the TOSCA spectrometer at ISIS.
## Density functional theory calculations
To study $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ systems from rst principles, we used density functional theory (DFT) within the generalized gradient approximation of Perdew, Burke, and Ernzerhof (PBE)56 and a van der Waals dispersion-corrected functional (vdW-DF2).57 The VASP program package58 with a planewave basis set and projector augmented wave59 pseudopotentials was used for all calculations. A Hubbard-like $U$ parameter60 was employed to better treat the localized d-states of the transition metal cations. We used an energy cutoff of 1000 eV for the planewave basis set and Brillouin zone sampling at the $T$ -point, leading to binding energies that converged to within $1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . Previous works have studied $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ systems at a variety of levels of theory,61–67 although typically the focus of these studies has been conned to some subset of the systems investigated here61–65 or has not been directed towards binding geometries.61,65,66 We nd reasonable quantitative agreement between our data and the only previous study addressing geometries and encompassing all $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ systems studied here.67
To generate binding geometries, we rst optimized periodic $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ crystals in a triclinic primitive unit cell of 54 atoms using $\mathrm{PBE}+U_{\mathrm{:}}$ , beginning from the experimental $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ structure and substituting in other divalent metal cations $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},$ ， Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu) at all metal sites, until the residual forces were smaller than 0.01 eV $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}$ and the stress tensor components were smaller than 0.2 kbar. The computed lattice parameters are all within $2\%$ of experiment. For open-shell cations, we employed Hubbard-like $U$ corrections. Values of $U$ used were 4.0, 4.0, 3.3, 6.4, and $4.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu, respectively; these values were taken from ref. 68, where they were determined for each metal cation to reproduce the experimental oxidation energy of MO to $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ . For all $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ analogs, we nd a high-spin ground state, and we assumed ferromagnetic ordering along the metal-oxide chain direction and antiferromagnetic ordering between the chains, as observed for the ground state magnetic ordering in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ .69 To predict adsorption geometries, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was relaxed inside rigid periodic $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ frameworks using vdW-DF2 $+\ U$ at a loading of one molecule per six metal sites. vdW-DF2 was shown in previous work to provide excellent binding energetics for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption to $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ systems.64 vdW-DF2 $+\ U$ was also used in single-point calculations to compute the energies of the bare $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ frameworks for use in calculating binding enthalpies.
Binding enthalpies at $308\mathrm{~K~}$ were calculated by including quantum zero-point energies (ZPEs) and nite-temperature thermal energies (TEs) at the level of a harmonic approximation. The enthalpy of adsorption of a molecule in a MOF is calculated as:
$$
-\Delta H(T)=H(T)(\mathrm{CO_{2}}+\mathrm{MOF})-H(T)(\mathrm{CO_{2}})-H(T)(\mathrm{MOF}),
$$
where $H(T)(\mathbf{MOF}),\ H(T)(\mathbf{CO_{2}}) $ , and $H(T)(\bf C O_{2}\cdot\bf M O F)$ are the enthalpies (at temperature $T$ ) of the bare MOF without $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the gas phase, and the MOF with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed, respectively.
## Results and discussion
## $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties
Low-pressure $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were collected for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ at 298, 308, and $318\mathrm{K}$ , as shown in Fig. 2. The steep initial rise in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake, likely due to strongly polarizing adsorption sites, is a feature oen observed in the presence of coordinatively-unsaturated metal centers. The adsorption capacity at 1.1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ of 7.2 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ corresponds to a value slightly higher than expected for $1\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II) site and implies additional contributions from secondary adsorption sites. The isosteric heat $(-Q_{s t})$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was calculated by tting the adsorption isotherms at each temperature with a dual-site Langmuir–Freundlich equation (see ESI† for details). At a loading of $0.1\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II), the heat of adsorption was determined to be $33.2{\left(1\right)}\ \mathrm{kJ}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , as shown in the inset of Fig. 2. This value is signicantly smaller than that reported for $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}),^{34}$ which is expected when considering the soer nature of the iron(II) cation (ionic radii for $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}=0.74~\mathrm{pm}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}=0.66\ \mathrm{pm})$ and electrostatic nature of the adsorbate/ adsorbent interaction. These observations are supported quantitatively by our DFT calculations, which predict heats of adsorption (at 298 K) of 34.1 and $40.9\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ and $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ , respectively, at a loading of 1 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per 6 metal cations.
To correlate structure with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties for this isostructural series, Mg-, Mn-, Co-, Ni-, Cu-, and Zn-frameworks were synthesized and characterized in addition to $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ . While the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties of many of these materials have been previously reported, $\mathbf{16,34-36,67,70,}7\mathbf{1}$ there are some inconsistencies, likely due to variations in synthetic procedures and/or sample activation or handling, making it difficult to assess the accuracy of some reported results.71 Fig. 3a shows the adsorption isotherms obtained for the seven analogues in the $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ series at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$ . The data is plotted as mol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ rather than the typical units of mmol $\mathrm{co}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ sample to avoid the effects of slight variations in formula weight. In the lowpressure regime, there is a notable difference in the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties, indicating that the open metal site dominates the adsorption process. Although binding strengths at the primary adsorption site vary throughout the series, upon saturation of the metals at higher pressures or lower temperatures, it is expected that the isotherms will begin to resemble one another due to the isostructural nature of the frameworks. Indeed, approaching 1 bar, the capacity of the Mg, Ni, Fe, and Co frameworks are already very similar with ${\sim}1.1\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per metal(II) indicating that these materials are already approaching the saturation capacity of the primary adsorption site.
Aer tting each isotherm with a dual-site Langmuir– Freundlich equation, the isosteric heats of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption were calculated for each framework and show the following trend at a loading of $0.1\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ : $\mathbf{Mg}>\mathbf{Ni}>\mathbf{Co}>\mathbf{Fe}>\mathbf{Mn}>\mathbf{Zn}>\mathbf{Cu}$ (Fig. 3b), with $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values that range from 22.1(2) to $43.5(2)\mathrm{~kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (Table 1). In good agreement with experimentally determined heats of adsorption, DFT calculations show binding enthalpies, $-\Delta H_{\ddagger}$ , that range from 27.1 to $40.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at 0.167 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ . While the theoretically determined trend, $\mathbf{Mg}>\mathbf{Ni}$ $>\mathrm{Fe}>\mathbf{Mn}>\mathbf{Co}>\mathbf{Zn}>\mathbf{Cu}$ , is slightly different from the experimental one, these differences are only observed for intermediate adsorbents including Mn, Fe, and Co, which exhibit similarities in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding strength. A full comparison of our experimental data and the DFT results is provided in Table 1.
Based on the electrostatic nature of the interaction between the $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ cations and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , one might expect that the observed trend should follow that of the ionic radii, as it does in a series of M-BTT MOFs;72 however, signicant deviations are observed. A recent report by Yu et al.67 gives an explanation for the observed trend through a description of nuclear screening effects by $\mathbf{M}^{2+}\mathbf{d}$ -orbitals. Their rst-principles study reveals that the relative strength of the electrostatic interaction is dictated by the effective charge of the metal cations at the open coordination site where $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binds. The isosteric heats of adsorption are fairly continuous for Mn, Zn, and Cu, while the $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and Ni show an abrupt change between 0.7 and $0.8\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ (Fig. 3b), possibly implying that not all of the metal sites are accessible. This discontinuity also implies that there is a signicant difference in energetics associated with the primary and secondary adsorption sites of the strongly adsorbing frameworks. Further, as the coverage approaches 1 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ for Mg, Ni, Co, and Fe, the isosteric heats approach a similar value of ${\sim}32\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , indicative of a homogenous distribution of adsorption enthalpies of the secondary adsorption sites, a direct result of the similar adsorption environments across the isostructural series.
