in the channels, indicating that the materials were completely activated. Rietveld renement of the host framework, followed by Fourier difference analysis allowed the subsequent elucidation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ site positions and occupancies. The material was rst loaded with $0.5\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II) site at room temperature. Aer slowly cooling, the adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was less than was initially loaded, 0.350(6) $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II), due to a small fraction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ freezing in the gas line of the top-loading CCR. Comparing the bare framework to that of the rst loading reveals a decrease in unit cell volume from 4041.3(1) $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ to $4021.4(2)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , which results from compression of the $a$ and $b$ axes upon adsorption (Fig. 4). The overall reduction in unit cell volume is $0.5\%$ , a value that is slightly less than that previously reported for the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loaded $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ analog.73 At this low-loading, there is population of a single site, site I, that is in close proximity to the iron(II) center, revealing that the open metal site is indeed responsible for the high initial isosteric heat of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Fig. 5). The adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ has an angular orientation with respect to the framework surface, with an $\bf{M}\mathrm{-}\bf{O}\mathrm{-}\bf{C}$ angle of $106(2)^{\circ}$ , a direct result of weak, secondary van der Waals interactions with the framework ligand. The observed $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ distance is $2.29\big(3\big)\mathring{\mathrm{\A}}$ , a value that, within error of the experiment, is similar to those observed for the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and Ni analogs.73,74
Increasing the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ from 0.35 to 1.3 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II) leads to an increase in site I occupancy as well as population of a secondary adsorption site (Fig. 5). The site II $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is nearly orthogonal to site I and lies almost parallel to the framework surface. Both sites I and II are surrounded by three nearest neighbouring $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules exhibiting weak van der Waals type interactions, as indicated by long intermolecular distances that average around $2.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ (drawn as blue dashed lines in Fig. 6). Distances between site II $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the framework are all slightly above $3{\mathring{\textbf{A}}}$ , implying that the intermolecular interactions are likely aiding in the stabilization of the population of site II. Further, the population of site II is facilitated by an increase in the $\bf{M}\mathrm{-O-C}$ angle from $106(2)^{\circ}$ to $117.2\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left(8\aftergroup\egroup\right)^{\circ}$ , while there is little change in the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO_{2})}}$ distance, from $2.29\overset{}{\underset{}{=}}3\overset{\circ}{\mathbf{A}}$ to $2.27(1)\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ . The observed change in the $\bf{M}\mathrm{-O-C}$ angle, which is also supported by NMR experiments performed on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ by Kong et al.,75 might suggest that, while there is minimal consequence on the electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , maximizing the van der Waals contacts for site $\mathrm{II}$ is necessary for realizing population of the weaker adsorption site. Aer full population of the secondary adsorption site, it is expected that further $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption will occur in the channels away from the framework walls, as there appears to be no room remaining on the surface for population of a tertiary adsorption site, as demonstrated in the space lling model shown in Fig. S25.†
High-resolution NPD data were also collected for several other isostructural analogs, including Mg-, Mn-, Co-, Cu-, and $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(d o b d c)}$ . Data were rst collected on the bare frameworks, and then with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loadings ranging from 0.5 to $1.5\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per metal(II). The aforementioned issues with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ freezing in the gas line were alleviated through the design of a custom-made gas-loading collar that t securely to a bottom-loading CCR.
Diffraction data collected on the Cu analog was used to assess the extremely low enthalpy of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, nearly linear $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake, and signicantly lower $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity at 1 bar (Fig. 3). Data from the bare framework reveals no excess scattering density in the channels, indicating that the material has been fully activated; however, the local environment around the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ cation is highly distorted. An axial elongation (Jahn–Tellerlike) results in a $\mathrm{\bfCu}{-}\mathrm{\bfO}(1)$ bond of $2.441(8)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ as compared to the average $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}(1)$ distance of $2.14\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in the other $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ structures, and a slight twist in the metal bound carboxylate group that causes ${\bf O}(2)$ to point out away from the normal square planar arrangement of the equatorial O atoms and into the framework channel. These distortions are also captured in DFT calculations, which show a ${\mathrm{Cu-O}}(1)$ bond of $2.44\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , as compared to an average $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}(1)$ distance of 2.16 A˚ for all experimentally studied $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ analogs. For comparison, a structural overlay of the $\mathbf{MO}_{5}$ polyhedra obtained from analyses of NPD data is shown in Fig. $\mathbf{S}26.\dagger$ A combination of this distortion and the overall lower effective ionic charge of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion likely limits the approachability of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule. Further, inefficient packing of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ signicantly limits the amount that can adsorb on the framework surface.
From in situ diffraction data collected on $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ loaded with $0.5\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ per copper(II), three adsorption sites were identied (Fig. 7a). Two of these sites are located on the framework wall, while the tertiary site is found disordered inside the framework channel. Site I, found at the open metal, shows a signicant elongation in the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distance of $2.86(3)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.$ and a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ coordination angle of $117(1)^{\circ}$ . It should be noted that weak nuclear scattering density that results from weakly-bound/ slightly-disordered $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ initially resulted in a local minimum for the structural analysis of this framework. This initial model showed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in a parallel orientation with the framework wall, rather than bound to the open metal site, (Fig. ${\bf S}27\dagger.$ ). Aer further analysis a secondary adsorption site was identied that allowed stabilization of the expected model, with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at the open metal (Fig. 7b). A comparison of the calculated diffraction pattern obtained from the structural model and the neutron diffraction data (Fig. 7c) reveals good agreement.
In addition to showing better agreement with the diffraction data, the new structural model also shows excellent agreement with the structural predictions from our DFT calculations (Fig. $S28\dagger$ ). Further, a nudged elastic band76 transition state method was used to further assess the potential existence of a kinetic barrier that might inhibit immediate adsorption at the open metal site and give rise to the existence of the initial structural model. The results show a monotonic decrease in energy (Fig. $^{\mathrm{{S29}\dagger}}$ ), as the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ moves from the less stable structure (Fig. ${\bf S}27\dagger$ ) to the more stable one (Fig. 7b), further indicating that no such barrier exists. As a last measure for further verication, high intensity X-ray diffraction data were additionally collected on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ at the APS. The rened structural model further supports the nal model obtained from NPD (Fig. S30 and Table S26†).
In previous studies, with other small molecules, such as ${\bf D}_{2}$ , we have shown that there is a direct correlation between isosteric heats of adsorption and metal–adsorbate distances.77 However, this experiment reveals that Mg, Ni, Fe, and Co have the same $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distances within the error of the experiment (Table 1), despite signicant variations in their $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values. It should be noted that the diffraction data were collected at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ , while the isosteric heats of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption were calculated from isotherms collected at temperatures above $298\mathrm{K}$ . The difference in experimental conditions could result in slight variations in the local framework environment around $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites, leading to a difference in the observed correlations. However, Debye–Waller factors for room temperature powder diffraction data can signicantly reduce the amount of structural detail extracted from the data if measured at higher temperatures. As such, most of the structural analysis reported here was carried out at low temperatures of ${\sim}10\ \mathrm{K}$ .
While the Mg-, Ni-, Fe-, and $\mathbf{Co-O(CO_{2})}$ distances range from 2.23(4) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ to $2.29(3)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ the Cu, Mn, and Zn analogs have elongated $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO_{2})}}$ distances of $2.86(3)\mathring{\mathrm{A}},2.51\big(3\big)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and $2.43(4)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ , respectively, which are in agreement with the lower isosteric heats of adsorption for the Cu-, Mn- and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -frameworks and the signicantly larger ionic radius of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ . It should be noted that while angles between the site I $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the framework vary slightly from material to material, there is no correlation observed between strength of binding and the M–O–C angles, as was suggested in a previous report by Yu et al. Further, there is population of a similar secondary adsorption site for all materials within the series at higher loadings, with an increase in the M–O–C angle observed in each instance. However, there again is no observed correlation between the strength of binding and $\bf{M}\mathrm{-}\bf{O}\mathrm{-}\bf{C}$ angle. Our DFT calculations agree well with our experimental observations, as shown in Table 1. Our calculations corroborate the conclusion that the $\bf{M}\mathrm{-}\bf{O}\mathrm{-}\bf{C}$ angles do not necessarily correlate with increasing binding strength. Particularly, we see a deviation from this trend in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ and $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ , suggesting that $\bf{M}\mathrm{-}\bf{O}\mathrm{-}\bf{C}$ angle cannot necessarily be used to predict binding strength. Additionally, our measured and calculated $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO_{2})}}$ distances agree well relative to one another, with DFT consistently overestimating the distances, in line with the known behavior of vdW-DF2. The exception to this is $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , for which our experimentally measured and calculated $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distances agree very well. The unexpected better agreement in this case is likely the consequence of secondary interactions with neighboring $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which could induce an elongation in the $\mathbf{Cu-O(CO_{2})}$ distance due to the overall weak interaction at the metal.
For the strongest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbents, including Mg-, Ni-, Fe-, and $\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , no difference is observed in the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O}}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ distances within error of the powder diffraction experiment. In order to see if a more highly resolved structural model could be obtained, additional single crystal studies were carried out on $\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , as this is the only compound in the series, other than $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , for which sizeable single crystals have been grown. Using a custom-designed gas cell and manifold, we dosed an activated single crystal with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . The X-ray data collected at $150\mathrm{~K~}$ indicate a $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distance of 2.261(9) A˚ and an angle of $171(6)^{\circ}$ (Table 1). While the data do not show signicant improvement in the rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ distances and angles, they do exhibit consistency with the values obtained from powder neutron diffraction at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ . For comparison, an overlay of the two structures is provided in Fig. S20.†
## The O–C–O bond angle of adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$
The rened $_{\mathbf{O-C-O}}$ angle of $179(3)^{\circ}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ is within error of the expected linear geometry of a free $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule, and, importantly, is signicantly larger than previous reports for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed in the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and Ni analogs $(164\div6)^{\circ}$ and $162\textcircled{3}^{\circ}$ , respectively), as obtained from both powder neutron73 and X-ray diffraction.74 Molecular bending has been of strong interest, as activation of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule can indicate signicant charge transfer between the adsorbed guest and framework, making strong implications towards chemical activation. However, bending could also result from signicant static/dynamic disorder in the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule that results in a misinterpretation of the bond angle from the time and position averaged diffraction experiments. Given the high-spin nature of the $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ cations in $\mathbf{M}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ , signicant charge transfer is not expected and theoretical studies suggest that large deviations from the expected linear geometry will require a signicant energy cost.78 Further, dynamic disorder is not likely at the data collection temperature of $10~\mathrm{K}.$ . Therefore, it is expected that the large bending in previous reports is related to static disorder, an idea that is further supported by the large displacement parameters observed for the two $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ atoms that are not directly coordinated to the metal.73 The rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ angle for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ indicates that if there is activation of the adsorbed gas molecule, it cannot be seen within error of the powder diffraction experiment.
It should also be noted that the diffraction data obtained from the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ (Fig. S16†), relative to the $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ previously studied, has much better data quality at higher angles or smaller $d$ -spacings, from which more accurate structural detail can be determined. It is thought that a combination of cooling the sample slowly in the presence of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ as a means of eliminating static disorder $(1\mathrm{~K~min}^{-1})$ and improving the crystallinity of the sample have signicantly aided us in the accuracy of the rened angles, compared to those previously reported for the Ni and Mg frameworks.73,74
Given the stronger $\mathbf{Mg-CO}_{2}$ interaction relative to Fe, we also varied the reaction conditions for the formation of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ as a means to improve the crystallinity (Fig. $^{\mathrm{S18\dagger}}$ , and again slowly cooled the sample in the presence of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Here again, there is a signicant improvement in the quality of the rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ angle for $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}),179(2)^{\circ}$ , for a loading of $0.8\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per metal, a value that is for the rst time much closer to those that have been predicted in this work (Table 1), and in previous DFT calculations.64,67,78 This observation is supported both by our work and recent studies by Yu et al. and Poloni et al. who use several computational methods to predict that, while the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is no longer linear, the intramolecular angle is at least larger than $174^{\circ}$ upon adsorption in $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ . This results from a largely electrostatic interaction between the metal cation of the framework and the adsorbed guest species.64,67 It should be further noted that for all of the materials in this study there is limited bending observed in the metal-bound $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with angles that range from $174(4)^{\circ}$ to $180(2)^{\circ}$ (Table 1).
## Temperature-dependent structural studies
Diffraction data collected on a high ux neutron powder diffractometer allowed determination of the temperaturedependent properties of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed within $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ between 20 and $300~\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. 8). The sample, loaded with 0.5 and then $1.5\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II), allowed a concerted evaluation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption level, as well as a determination of the site occupation. At a loading of $0.5\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II), there is population only at site I, without signicant desorption occurring until ${\sim}200~\mathrm{K}$ If there is any population of site II during the heating process, it cannot be seen within the error of the experiment. For the 1.5 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II) loading, there is population of both sites with signicant amounts of desorption again above $200\ \mathrm{K}.$ While there is minimal population of site II at room temperature, it should be noted that this occupancy is not zero, with a $5:1$ ratio found for the population of sites I and II, respectively. The equilibrium pressure was monitored during the heating process (Fig. ${\bf S}22{\dagger}$ ), and at room temperature it was found to be 0.65 bar with the total population of sites I and II, equivalent to approximately $0.77~\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per iron(II). The total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ occupancy correlates well with the results obtained from the isotherm at $298~\mathrm{K},$ , as shown in Fig. 3a.
Since the binding energy associated with adsorption at site II is thought to be somewhat similar across the series, it is expected that materials that bind $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ more strongly will exhibit a much larger contribution from site I $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at low pressures and room temperature. For a qualitative comparison to the Fe analog, room-temperature neutron diffraction data were collected for $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(d o b d c)}$ . The data, collected at an equilibrium pressure around 0.65 bar, reveal an approximate $3:1$ ratio for occupancies in site I and II, respectively, and a total occupancy of $0.62\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per zinc(II). The room temperature data collection reveals a slight elongation of the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O}}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ distance and also a rearrangement of the secondary adsorption site that makes the intermolecular distances entirely too short for the two sites to be simultaneously occupied. Therefore, we consider that the overall weaker interaction at the open metal will not allow stabilization of the secondary adsorption site, and this directly correlates to less efficient packing of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules on the framework surface relative to the other analogs, except for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ . As in the case of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ , the rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ occupancy for $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(d o b d c)}$ correlates well with that determined from the isotherms collected at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 3). In contrast to the roomtemperature results for Fe and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}$ , those previously obtained from $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ treated under similar conditions reveal no occupancy of site II within the error of the diffraction experiment and a nearly full occupancy of site I.72 These results are not surprising, as occupation ratios are expected to increase with larger differences in binding energy of site I and II, as predicted by a Boltzmann distribution.
## Inelastic neutron scattering
INS was used to probe the low-energy vibrational modes associated with adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ (Fig. ${\bf S}24\dagger$ ) using the FANS spectrometer. Data were rst collected on the bare framework and then again with the sample loaded with $0.75\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ per iron(II). A subtraction of the data (Fig. S24b†) collected for the bare sample was then performed to search for neutron energy loss related to vibrational or bending modes of the surface bound $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ ; however, the difference spectrum reveals negative and positive intensities, indicating signicant shis associated with the observed framework modes upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. This is not surprising, as this framework exhibits a signicant reduction in overall unit cell volume upon gas adsorption (Fig. 4), indicating that the bound $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can easily perturb framework phonon frequencies. Both soening and hardening of the vibrational modes are observed, likely due to the inverse effect $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption has on the lengths of the $a/b.$ - and $c$ -axes, as previously noted from the diffraction data.
Additionally, it is expected that the intensity related to vibrational modes of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ should be relatively weak compared to framework modes, since the incoherent scattering cross sections for C and O, relative to those of H, are weak. As a result, INS spectra were also collected for the $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc}\cdot d_{2})$ framework using the TOSCA spectrometer. The goal was to improve both the resolution of the spectra and achieve a lower contribution to the background spectrum from H atoms in the framework. However, despite deuteration of the framework ligand and the higher resolution instrument, there is still no convincing indication of vibrational modes associated with framework adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and no splitting of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ wag mode around $80{\mathrm{~meV}}$ .
## Surface areas
Langmuir surface areas of the six $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ compounds were determined through 77 K ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements (Fig. S6†). Comparisons made on a per gram basis (Table 2) immediately imply differences in the as-prepared materials. These problems are not resolved when taking variations in formula weight of the series into consideration, as the a Langmuir surface areas were rst converted to $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ basis to account for differences in formula weight and then divided by the molar surface area of $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ to obtain the surface area ratio.
normalized Langmuir surface area of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ is still nearly $24\%$ less than that of $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ . Further, for all of the analogs including Mg, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu, the number of ${\bf N}_{2}$ per unit cell range from 39 to $45\ \Nu_{2}$ at 1 bar, while $z\mathrm{n}$ is signicantly lower at $35~\Nu_{2}$ per unit cell. There has been some debate over reasons for the overall lower surface area associated with $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(d o b d c)}$ , relative to the other analogs.16 Possible reasons could be related to any or a combination of the following: structural changes in the framework, the presence of an amorphous impurity, remaining ligand/solvent blocking the pores or open metal sites, or crystalline defects such as intergrowth that can block $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ access to the channels.
The structural studies carried out here have been used to address some of these questions. Firstly, Fourier difference analysis of the diffraction data collected on the bare framework reveals no residual scattering density in the channels indicating that there is no remaining solvent at the open metal. Secondly, there is no evidence of a structural distortion around Zn or in the rest of the framework that might limit accessibility at lowtemperatures (Fig. $^{\mathrm{S21\dag}}.$ ). Thirdly, it is expected that the presence of an amorphous phase would inadvertently cause a disagreement in the amount of dosed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ versus the rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ occupancy; however, Rietveld renement of the $10\mathrm{~K~}$ diffraction data indicates that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ occupancies, $0.54(2)\ C\mathrm{O}_{2}$ per zinc(II), closely mirror the amount loaded, $0.5\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ , supporting the idea that there is likely no amorphous phase in this sample. Lastly, it might be attractive to draw correlations between the unit cell volumes of the activated $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ series with the determined surface areas. However, unit cell volumes reveal the following trend: $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}>\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}>\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}>\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}>\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}>\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ (Fig. 4). The copper(II) analog is an outlier, exhibiting signicantly lower cell volume relative to zinc(II) yet a high surface area; this result suggests that there is no correlation between these two variables and cannot be evaluated further without assessment of the structural characteristics of ${\bf N}_{2}$ binding within the framework.
At higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loadings, $1.5\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per zinc(II), the gas began condensing as the sample was cooled. The rened $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ occupancy in the $10\mathrm{~K~}$ data revealed a maximum achievable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading of approximately $1.29\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per zinc(II), with a site I occupancy of approximately 0.69(2). This data indicates that a large fraction of open-metal sites, approximately $30\%$ , are not accessed, as the sample has clearly reached a maximum capacity at a signicantly lower loading than the other analogs. There are clearly fewer accessible metal sites in the compound, which could be the result of crystalline defects. However, Zn is one of the few analogs, with the exception of Co, for which we have thus far been successful in growing sizeable single crystals, implying that there might be some signicant difference in the nucleation and growth processes relative to the other derivatives. A recent study has shown that the growth and nucleation rates for Zn are signicantly larger than those of Co or Ni independent of the synthetic technique and that higher nucleation and growth rates have an inverse effect on pore volume.79 When considering all of the information at hand, one cannot rule out that crystalline defects could give rise to lower surface areas and pose limitations on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.
## Conclusions
The foregoing results reveal the rst full experimental study of both the structural properties and gas adsorption behavior within an extensive isostructural series of MOFs of the type $\mathbf{M}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ . Unlike previous studies, we show that intramolecular angles of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed at the metal cations exhibit minimal deviations from $180^{\circ}$ . This nding lends more awareness of the framework properties required to achieve $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ activation, information that is pertinent to ongoing discussions regarding chemical conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Additionally, by computing binding structures and energetics using DFT, we have found excellent agreement with the experimental results. Powder neutron and single crystal X-ray diffraction data reveal minimal differences between the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed frameworks for $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g} $ Fe, or Co, despite significant differences in isosteric heats of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Larger decreases in the strength of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding at the open metal site leads to an elongated metal– ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O}({\bf C O}_{2})$ distance for Mn, Cu, and Zn. The Cu analog additionally reveals an axial elongation that also leads to a ligand oxygen atom positioned so as to inhibit the approach of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to the open metal site. $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ further has two adsorption sites of comparable binding strength, as evidenced by similarities in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ site occupancy, even at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ . Assessment of room temperature diffraction and adsorption data reveals that a secondary adsorption site does contribute to the uptake properties of many of these materials.
This study has further drawn attention to possible crystalline defects associated with the $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ series. Both structural analysis and adsorption measurements indicate that for some of the analogs, in particular $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ , a large percentage of the open metal sites are inaccessible, an occurrence that likely stems from intergrowth and can seriously limit performance. As such, this study might warrant further investigation of crystallization processes within the whole series in order to understand correlations between reaction conditions and sample quality. It is our hope that in-depth experimental efforts like this one can provide the insight necessary for the eventual deliberate design of new materials for energetically favorable carbon capture technologies.
